    weekNumber: 22,
    weekArc: "The Judge",
    weekArcLatin: "Iudex",
    title: "Solomon and the Two Mothers",
    scripture: {
      reference: "3 Kings 3:24-27 (DR)",
      text: "And the king said: Bring me a sword. And when they had brought a sword before the king, Divide, said he, the living child in two, and give half to the one and half to the other. But the woman whose child was alive, said to the king (for her bowels were moved upon her child): I beseech thee, my lord, give her the child alive, and do not kill it. But the other said: Let it be neither mine nor thine, but divide it. The king answered, and said: Give the living child to this woman, and let it not be killed, for she is the mother thereof.",
    },
    excerpt: {
      author: "St. Augustine of Hippo",
      citation: "Sermons on Selected Lessons of the New Testament, Sermon X (on the judgment of Solomon)",
      text: "Behold the wisdom of Solomon, that he might find the mother. He saw two women claiming, and one heart only could be the mother's. He used the sword not to divide the child but to divide the women. The false one was content that the child should perish; the true one preferred to lose her right rather than her child. The judge sought not the legal proof but the love that proved itself in pity. Brethren, when ye judge, remember that the heart of the mother is hidden, and only by looking for love will ye find the truth.",
    },
    meditation:
      "There is no surviving deed of sale, no eyewitness, no DNA test, no midwife in the room. Two women, one living child, one dead. Both claim. Both stand the same height before the throne. The case, on the record alone, is unresolvable. Solomon does what no modern bench would dare and what no procedural rule would permit: he orders the child cut in half. The order is, on its face, monstrous. It is also one of the most celebrated acts of judgment in the literature of the world. The Fathers from Origen onward read it as the type of the just judge — and Augustine, preaching it to his African congregation, refuses to let the story be reduced to a clever trick.\n\nThe king is not bluffing. That is the first thing to see. If he were bluffing, the story would be cynical: a magistrate playing chicken with an infant. Augustine reads it differently. The king has been given wisdom by God in the chapter just before — ask me whatever you will, the Lord said in the dream at Gibeon, and Solomon asked for an understanding heart to judge thy people, and to discern between good and evil. Solomon receives the gift and immediately is asked to use it. The sword is the gift's first instrument. It is real. It is drawn. It is held above a real baby in front of two real women. The judgment turns on what the sword reveals.\n\nIt reveals love. The false mother is content to let the child be divided — let it be neither mine nor thine — because she has no investment in his living. Her claim was about her status, not about the child; once the child is no longer an asset, she will gladly settle for the equality of mutual loss. The true mother breaks. Her bowels are moved upon her child, the Douay says, translating the Hebrew with the visceral word the Hebrew uses. She would rather lose the case than lose the boy. She tells the king to give the child to her rival. The king has his answer. Give the living child to this woman, and let it not be killed, for she is the mother thereof.\n\nWhat Augustine notices, and what bears sitting with for any lawyer, is that the just judgment is not arrived at by procedure alone. The procedure produced an impasse. The wisdom that broke the impasse was a wisdom about love — about who in this room actually loves the contested thing. The judge had to look past the claims to the loves underneath them. He had to design an act that would force the loves to show themselves. In doing so, he risked the very thing the loves were about; he held the sword above the child to make the love appear. That is not a procedural move. It is a deeper move. The Fathers will say it is a move of the gift of counsel, which Aquinas later treats as a gift of the Holy Spirit that perfects the virtue of prudence.\n\nA lawyer reads this and feels a particular discomfort. We have been trained to mistrust judges who depart from the record. The record is our friend; the record is what we built; the record is what protects the client from the personal opinion of the bench. The whole apparatus of evidence and procedure exists to make sure that the judgment is pronounced on the cause and not on the judge's intuition. We are right to want this. The Anglo-American adversarial system, properly run, is one of the more humane juridical inventions of the Christian centuries. It does not believe in Solomon. It believes in due process.\n\nAnd yet — and Augustine knows it — there are cases in which the record alone is not enough. The two-mothers case is one of them. The custody dispute where both parents look fine on paper and only one will fight for the child rather than the win. The eviction where both stories check out and only one of them is the story of someone who actually lived in the apartment. The contested will where the writings are ambiguous and the family conferences will reveal which child loved the deceased and which one was waiting. Every jurisdiction has a few cases a year in which a judge must do something a little like what Solomon did. He must design an inquiry that exposes the love.\n\nThis is, frankly, dangerous. Bad judges have used this license to do appalling things. Augustine knows it. He preaches the sermon to ordinary Christians, not to magistrates. His implicit warning is that you cannot imitate Solomon by imitating his procedure. You can only imitate him by being a person to whom God has given an understanding heart, which is the gift Solomon asked for and was praised for asking. The understanding heart is not a technique. It is a state of soul. It comes by prayer, by the long practice of looking past appearances, by a refusal to let the formal claim displace the deeper question of who actually loves the contested good.\n\nWhat is in this for the lawyer who will never sit on the bench? Something concrete. You spend much of your professional life trying to win for one of the two women. The system asks you to do that. Augustine, however, asks something underneath the system: when the case is before you, is the claim you are pressing the claim of the woman whose bowels are moved upon her child, or is it the claim of the other one? Sometimes you do not know until you are deep into the file. Sometimes the answer arrives late and inconveniently. The honest lawyer notices when the answer arrives. He does not always switch sides — there are other duties, and the client retained him — but he begins to plead differently. He stops asking for the whole child. He begins to look for the order that keeps the child alive, even if it means his client loses some.\n\nThis is what Solomon's wisdom gives the lawyer who is not a king: a habit of looking past the formal claim to the love beneath it, and of pleading in such a way that the love can come forward. It is a quiet way to practice. It does not always win. But the cases in which it has been deployed are the cases you will still remember at the end of your career, when the wins and losses on the docket have all blurred together.\n\nClose the day with the Gibeon prayer. Solomon, given the chance to ask for anything, asked for an understanding heart to judge thy people, and to discern between good and evil. The same prayer is open to you, in a smaller register, every morning before you open the first email. Ask for it.",
