    excerpt: {
      workId: "newman-idea",
      author: "St. John Henry Newman",
      citation: "A Letter Addressed to the Duke of Norfolk, §5 ('Conscience')",
      text: "Conscience is the aboriginal Vicar of Christ, a prophet in its informations, a monarch in its peremptoriness, a priest in its blessings and anathemas, and, even though the eternal priesthood throughout the Church could cease to be, in it the sacerdotal principle would remain and would have a sway. Conscience has rights because it has duties; but in this age, with a large portion of the public, it is the very right and freedom of conscience to dispense with conscience, to ignore a Lawgiver and Judge, to be independent of unseen obligations.",
    },
    meditation:
      "Paul, writing to a mixed congregation of Jews and Gentiles in Rome, makes an observation that should rearrange the way a lawyer hears the word conscience. The Gentiles do not have the Mosaic law. They have not been at Sinai; they have no scroll; they do not keep the feasts. And yet — Paul says — when they do by nature the things of the law, they show that the law is written in their hearts. Their conscience bears witness; their thoughts accuse or defend; the court is in session inside them.\n\nThe vocabulary Paul reaches for is forensic. Witness, accusation, defense, judgment. He could have used any vocabulary at all. He chose the courtroom's. The conscience, the Apostle says, is structured like a tribunal. There is the act, there is the testimony about the act, there is the prosecution by one set of thoughts and the defense by another, and there is the moment of ruling. The Greek word he uses for conscience, syneidesis, is itself a small juridical term — knowing-with, co-knowing, the witness who walks beside the act and remembers it. Latin renders it conscientia, with the same etymology. The con- is the courtroom. The whole self is in chambers.\n\nNewman, three centuries after Trent and one century after the Enlightenment, returned to this Pauline picture with a stubborn fierceness. Conscience, he insisted, is the aboriginal Vicar of Christ — the original deputy, the first authority by which Christ rules in a soul that has not yet been reached by Church or Scripture. It is a prophet, a monarch, a priest. To suppress it, or to redefine it as the right to do whatever one wants, is to dismantle the court God has built into the self.\n\nA lawyer ought to feel a strange kinship with this image. You spend your days in courtrooms. The conscience says: there is one more. It is not in the building you walk to in the morning. It is in the building you walk around in. It does not adjourn. It does not lose jurisdiction. There is no statute of limitations on the matters it hears, and there is no opposing counsel who can settle them for you in a hallway conference. You are the defendant, the witness, the prosecution, and the defense, and there is a judge sitting somewhere behind your sternum who is older than you are.\n\nThe practical question is whether you appear in that court the way you appear in the other one. You prepare for the courthouse. You read the rules; you check the citations; you brief the issue; you arrive early; you wear the suit. The inner tribunal does not get a tenth of that attention. Most lawyers walk past it for days at a time. The docket calls and no one shows. The thoughts begin to accuse, and you put on a podcast. The thoughts begin to defend, and you let the defense run unopposed. The conscience is in session whether you appear or not, and the rulings continue to be entered, but they are entered in your absence, against a defendant who has waived appearance.\n\nWhat would it look like to appear? The Catholic tradition has, for fifteen centuries, prescribed a practice for this and called it the examination of conscience. It is unsentimental. You sit, usually before bed, and you bring to mind the day. You do not generalize. You do not say I was probably impatient at some point. You name the moment. The associate whom you spoke to with a tone you would not have used to a client. The bench memo you let go out unchecked because you were tired. The look on your spouse's face at the dinner table when you were still on email. You bring it forward, you let the thought accuse, you let whatever defense is honest defend, and you let the inner judge rule. The ruling is not condemnation; that is not the chief work of this court. The chief work is recognition.\n\nIgnatius of Loyola taught a version of this practice — the daily Examen — that has become common across Christian traditions. Newman, who had read his Catholic forerunners carefully, names the same thing in different language: the conscience must be kept clean by daily inspection, because it is the seat of Christ's vicar in the soul, and a neglected vicariate becomes, in time, a deserted one. The conscience does not get destroyed by being ignored. It gets quieted. It still rules. The rulings just stop reaching the daylight.\n\nThis matters for the work because the legal life is unusually structured to silence conscience. The role of the advocate is to argue in the alternative, to take a position because it is the position one has been retained to take, to subordinate, professionally, the private opinion to the client's interest. Within proper limits, this is legitimate and even noble — the client deserves a representative whose private opinions about her cause do not bleed into the brief. Outside proper limits, it becomes a habit of thinking against one's own conscience for hire, and after enough years the inner judge cannot make himself heard through the static. The lawyer who has not appeared in the inner court for ten years will have a hard time, when the great test comes, remembering how to find the courtroom.\n\nPaul ends the passage with a clause that is easy to miss and worth lingering on: in the day when God shall judge the secret things of men, by Jesus Christ, according to my gospel. The inner court is not the final court. It is the rehearsal for it. Every honest appearance in the inner tribunal is a small participation in the final tribunal, and the verdict you let yourself hear from your own conscience tonight is a thin echo of the verdict that will be heard on the great day. Christians have not always been comfortable with this language. It is, however, the Apostle's. The judgment of the secret things is coming. The conscience is the court that is letting you settle the cases now, while there is still time to be reconciled to your adversary on the way.\n\nDo something practical tonight. Five minutes, before bed. Three questions. What did I do today that I would not want named? Whom did I wrong, even slightly, whom I have not yet sought out? What would my conscience say if I let it speak? Sit through the answers. Do not rush them. Then, if you are Catholic, say the act of contrition. If you are not, say what Newman called the soul's first prayer, the prayer the conscience teaches before any catechism does: God, be merciful to me, a sinner. Sleep. The inner court is in session. You have, at least, appeared.",
